# Durby Warehouse reverse proxy.
#
# Set FRONTEND_DOMAIN / API_DOMAIN in the root .env for production to a bare
# domain (e.g. warehouse.example.com / api.warehouse.example.com, no scheme)
# — Caddy will provision and renew Let's Encrypt certificates for them
# automatically. The defaults below are explicitly "http://" so that local
# `docker compose up` (FRONTEND_DOMAIN/API_DOMAIN left unset) serves plain
# HTTP with no TLS at all — no self-signed/internal-CA certificate, so
# there's nothing for a browser or `fetch`/`curl` to reject or warn about.

# Security headers, applied to both sites below.
# - X-Content-Type-Options / X-Frame-Options: unconditionally safe, no
#   dev/prod distinction needed.
# - Strict-Transport-Security: per RFC 6797 (and every major browser),
#   HSTS response headers are only honored when received over an already-
#   validated HTTPS connection — a browser silently ignores this header on
#   a plain-HTTP response. Local dev serves plain HTTP only (see the site
#   blocks below), so this is inert there and only takes effect once
#   production traffic is actually over HTTPS (which Caddy's automatic
#   Let's Encrypt cert provides for a real domain). Nothing to toggle.
# - No Content-Security-Policy here: the frontend and API are served from
#   different origins/subdomains in production (and locally), so a
#   same-origin-only CSP (script-src/connect-src 'self') would break the
#   frontend's cross-origin API calls — getting that right needs per-origin
#   templating this config doesn't have, so it's deliberately left out
#   rather than shipped half-correct. frame-ancestors is covered by
#   X-Frame-Options above instead, which needs no origin list.
(security_headers) {
	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		X-Frame-Options DENY
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
	}
}

{$FRONTEND_DOMAIN:http://localhost} {
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy frontend:80
}

{$API_DOMAIN:http://api.localhost} {
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy backend:3000
}
